<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vima Kadphises</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vima Kadphises was a Kushan emperor of the late 1st and early 2nd centuries CE, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vima-takto">Vima Takto</a> and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription">Rabatak inscription</a>. He extended Kushan rule deep into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and introduced a large gold coinage, much of it showing the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oesho">Oesho</a> with the bull Nandi. His reign shifted the weight of the empire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> towards India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vima-kadphises.jpg" alt="Vima Kadphises" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Double_stater_Vima_Kadphises_Guimet_MG24356_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
